window.COACH = {"page": "software", "title": "Digital Twin", "prizeName": "Best Software, Best Measurement", "summary": {"verdict": "The page is much closer to a winner's page than it was on 12 October. It is about a quarter shorter at roughly 6,230 words, it runs in six sections, and four things the first review said a Best Software judge could not find are on it: what the three programs are, how to run the rig app, what the log contains and what licence the code carries. Most of the contradictions found in the last round are fixed, including the cuvette path, run 1's readings and the plate prior, which now says \"whether it transfers to the reactor has not been tested\". On validation it is still ahead of the winners we read, with a baseline detector, a walk-forward forecast and a leave-one-arm-out table. Two things hold it back. The code has no address a judge can clone and the iGEM GitLab mirror is still a promise, which is an eligibility matter. And the protocols are unfinished: the TiO2 run, the pressure sweep and the trust layer's score cannot yet be repeated from the page. By a rough count of the live HTML about two thirds of the words are the instructor's; the students' own sentences are mostly in R2 to R6, and that is where the praise below sits.", "strengths": ["An argument in one sentence. \"Without manual sampling, the reactor's own instruments are the only record of the batch, and they drift as they wear\" sets up everything after it, and it is a student sentence.", "Validation against something. R3 scores the trust layer against a plain step detector on the same data, R5 scores the Kalman filter against persistence at four horizons with a walk-forward rule stated in one line, and R6 holds out each light arm in turn. No winning software page we read compares its tool to a simpler method.", "Measurement reporting in the newest sections. R4 gives its sites, runs, interval and 540 paired readings before any result and reports the wetness channel as untested. The silica repeat gives a protocol in one sentence and a dilute-and-restore check with SD and n at each level.", "Limits placed beside the results they limit. R5 says it \"forecasts the photometer reading, not biomass\" directly under its table, R3 says it has been shown \"on one run with one event of each kind\", R6 says the transfer to the reactor has not been tested, and the page applies its own definition to itself and calls the system a digital shadow."], "priorities": ["Put the code where a judge can get it. Make the iGEM GitLab mirror live and print its full address in the abstract, in section 4 and in section 6. Then name the script, the log and the command behind the trust layer, so \"the 434 h batch comes with it as a test case\" is something a reader can run.", "Finish the protocols. The TiO2 run still lacks its step size, its liquid, its blank and its wait. R1 lacks the hold time per pump setting. R3 never says what the score on either axis is, or when the 120-hour window was chosen. The photometer has a ceiling and no stated floor, on a page where two runs start near zero.", "Settle the two facts that still disagree across pages. R2 says the photometer's \"response per milligram falls\" while Measurement says \"the BioDrop is slightly less linear than our own photometer\". The containment bullet says a broken fibre \"shows up\" in trans-membrane pressure, the limits say that pressure is \"below transducer resolution at the operating point\", and Safety says monitoring \"ensures early detection\".", "Give the reader what stands behind four sentences that stay as written. Say which sites \"1.2 to 1.9 °C\" covers, what \"28 times slower\" is measured against, that expression in the green arms was not confirmed, and that the introduction describes a design. Put \"replayed\" in the trust layer's ledger row and OD in place of \"biomass\" in the plate prior's.", "Help a judge use the software. Add a numbered walkthrough for one interface and a figure of the three parts, say who wrote the rewrite and the analysis scripts, and report whether anyone outside the dry lab has run any of it."], "ballot": [{"question": "How well is the software compatible with, and does it leverage, existing synthetic biology standards? (e.g. SBOL, other RFCs, data formats, etc.)", "status": "partial", "where": "2 What the reactor measures now; 4 What survives which failure", "note": "The page says the log is plain CSV with a fixed 73-column header and follows no standard such as SBOL, and it names 26 of the columns; the six provenance tags are labels on the page and only temperature carries its source in the log."}, {"question": "How well was the software validated (e.g., through experimental work, test data, or benchmarking)?", "status": "strong", "where": "3 Results (R3, R5, R6)", "note": "A baseline detector, a walk-forward comparison and a leave-one-arm-out table are ahead of the winners we read, with two caveats: each rests on one run or one plate, and the trust layer's score and window are not yet defined on the page."}, {"question": "Can the software be useful to other projects?", "status": "partial", "where": "6 For other teams", "note": "Seven transferable methods are offered and one comes with a dataset, but no file, script or command is named and no user outside the team is reported."}, {"question": "How well can the software be integrated with external tools/software applications? (APIs, packages, etc.)", "status": "partial", "where": "6 For other teams (serial protocol); 4 What survives which failure (log)", "note": "Two lines of the serial protocol and the log's column groups are shown; the protocol belongs to the rewrite, which has measured nothing, and its fields carry no names or units."}, {"question": "Is the software user-friendly?", "status": "partial", "where": "4 The software that runs the reactor", "note": "Two live embeds, an English and Traditional Chinese interface and a normal range behind every information mark, against no walkthrough of either interface and no account of anyone else using it."}, {"question": "How well is the software written and documented for future groups to extend and improve? (Code documentation, comments in the code, performance evaluation, architecture diagrams, installation and execution scripts, etc.)", "status": "partial", "where": "4 The software that runs the reactor", "note": "A three-part description with file names, a run route and a Licence card are there; still missing are a clone address, the live GitLab mirror, an architecture figure, an English README and any description of tests."}, {"question": "Could the measurement(s) be reproduced by other iGEM teams?", "status": "partial", "where": "3 Results (R2 silica repeat, R4)", "note": "The silica repeat and the leaf sensor bench test can be repeated from the page; R1 lacks its hold time, the TiO2 run lacks step, liquid, blank and wait, and R3's score is never defined."}, {"question": "Are the methods or protocols described well?", "status": "partial", "where": "R2 The silica repeat; R4", "note": "Two sections read as protocols and the TiO2 run carries its doses and volume, but the page's first calibration is still half a protocol."}, {"question": "Could it be useful to other projects?", "status": "strong", "where": "6 For other teams", "note": "Give every sensor a companion that cannot see what it measures, shown twice on this page on two different instruments, is the most reusable idea here."}, {"question": "Did the team validate and calibrate their measurement process appropriately for their approach?", "status": "partial", "where": "R2, R3, R4", "note": "Calibration against a known dose, a dilute-and-restore check and a sensor bias reported in the section that uses the sensor are strong; against them stand no lower limit of detection and a disagreement with Measurement about which instrument is the less linear."}], "skeleton": {"releaf": ["1 Introduction", "2 What the reactor measures now", "3 Results (Runs, R1 to R6)", "4 The software that runs the reactor", "5 Gaps and limits", "6 For other teams"], "winners": ["What it is and who needs it", "Why it exists, with a number", "Architecture, components with their jobs", "Method or model per component", "Install: requirements, then commands, then the URL you end up with", "Usage: a numbered walkthrough or a worked example", "Validation", "Limits and future work", "Repository, licence, references"], "gap": "Section 4 carries components, a run route and the log format in prose, which closes most of the gap the first review found. Three winner sections are still absent: a usage section with a numbered walkthrough, an architecture figure, and a repository line with an address. The order also differs. Winners say what the tool is and how to run it before they validate it; here the results come first, so a software judge meets the software at section 4."}, "extras": ["BR-01 is never introduced. The abstract opens \"BR-01 has grown B. subtilis four times\" and the introduction says \"Local data from BR-01\". Write \"our perfusion bioreactor, BR-01\" at first use in each.", "\"This is the rig as designed.\" sits under a table in a section headed \"What the reactor measures now\". Move it above the table, as you did for the field sensors with \"Today's rig software reads none of these channels.\"", "\"In the log only temperature carries its source, in temp_src; the columns are under What survives which failure.\" The second half points at a fold title in another section. Write \"the column list is in section 4\".", "\"which justifies using the model as a soft sensor for flow\": flow is measured by the turbine meter and tagged in-line, and the soft channels in your table are lumen ΔP, TMP and fouling ratio. The 1.03 agreement is also at one flow, with \"No point was held out.\" Say what the model is used for and where.", "\"Axial non-uniformity is 28% against a 20% target\": the term is never defined and the target has no source. Give each a clause.", "\"Past OD 1.2 the linear fit's error rises from 0.0235 to 0.1715\" follows \"residual SD 0.024 OD\". Name the error measure, give the number of points, and use one precision for both.", "\"enough time for a controller to drop the faulty feed\": on a perfusion reactor \"feed\" reads as medium. Write \"to stop using the OD reading\".", "\"flagged 23% of sensor anomalies and 0.8% of biological shifts\" has no counts behind the percentages, and the table's \"35–36%\" is a range for a single share. Give the denominators.", "\"settles kLa (45.7 against 60 h⁻¹)\": neither number has a source on any page, and Biomanufacturing says the culture \"needs a kLa of 12.5 h−1\". Say what 45.7 and 60 are estimates of. \"Neither probe can be autoclaved\" also needs the two probes named, because the channel table says \"None fitted\".", "\"The probe is fitted but has not been answering, so the app runs on a typed temperature.\" Every reactor temperature on the page, including \"434 hours at 22 °C\", is therefore a set value. Say so once, in the run table.", "\"the rig app records and the rewrite is tested on its recordings\": say whether that is an automated test or a recording loaded by hand, because a judge reads \"tested\" as the first.", "\"The log is plain CSV with a fixed 73-column header\" is followed by 26 column names. Write \"the main columns are\" and link the full header. Name run 3's nine columns too, since run 3 is the dataset you offer other teams.", "The module has two geometries across our pages and the run table does not say which was fitted when. This page: \"11 fibres of 1 mm bore and 0.6 m length, 0.02 m²\". Hardware's bioreactor page: \"Fiber area 150 cm² Fibers 8\". Engineering has the reason: \"The module frozen on 4 July is 150 cm² across eight fibres; the module characterised on 16 August is 200 cm² across eleven.\" Add the module to each run's set-up.", "Figure 1's caption uses three date formats for its sources: \"260610 test1 no stir.xlsx\", \"0721, test3 20 day.csv\" and \"20260902\". Run 1 is also dated 6 June in the table (\"prototype 260606\") while its file is 260610. Keep the file names and add a note on the date.", "\"Valves move under software control\" in the limits: the channel table has one commanded pinch valve and four Luer valves \"Turned by hand; no actuator yet\". Write \"The pinch valve moves under software control\".", "\"Beer–Lambert at scale.\" is the only limit heading that does not say what the limit is. \"Linear range of the photometer\" would.", "\"The SHT31 matched the nominal temperature of each site.\" Say where each nominal value comes from, so the reader knows the air probe was checked against something other than itself.", "The key results row \"Reads 1.2 to 1.9 °C warm against the same air; wetness untested\" should name the two sites it covers once the R4 sentence does.", "The light array has three names across our pages. This page says \"light-programmable array (LPA)\"; Dry Lab Notebook says \"ran them on DiOPAL in the incubator\" and \"the 19 September OptoLPA ACCD run\". Use one name, or give the others once in brackets.", "The live weather source differs from Math Model. This page: \"Open-Meteo, hourly, five days back and three ahead\". Math Model: \"Central Weather Administration (中央氣象署) station data for live use\". If these are two feeds for two jobs, say so on both pages.", "Engineering still says the reference channel \"flagged the window as untrustworthy while it was happening\". This page now says \"The alarm was computed afterwards from the log\". This page has it right; ask Engineering to match.", "The leaf sensor write-up links to \"igem2026-leaf-sensor-bench\", a repository outside iGEM's GitLab. Include it in the mirror with the rest.", "References 4, 5 and 6 are never cited in the text. Cite them where they are used or remove them."]}, "live": "https://2026.igem.wiki/gems-taiwan/", "mockPages": ["model", "software", "protein-design", "protein-design/methodology", "protein-design/sprints", "protein-design/molecular-dynamics", "hardware/bioreactor", "hardware/photometer", "hardware/diopal", "hardware/hydroponics"], "wiki": "0a3583e, 2026-10-13", "root": "../", "notes": [{"id": "dt-a11", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "so every reading here shows when it has stopped being true", "title": "The first line promises every reading", "body": "This is a strong opening and it claims more than the page shows. The next sentences say four results carry a second check, and only the OD channel has a reference beam; pH has no companion and the loop temperature is typed in by hand. Narrow the promise to the question the page asks of each reading.", "after": "A reactor that runs for weeks with nobody beside it is only as good as its own record, so this page asks of each reading how we would know it had stopped being true.", "excerpts": [], "zone": "abstract"}, {"id": "dt-a05", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "Both interfaces run live on this page under the MIT licence", "title": "Say where the code is", "body": "The licence is now in the abstract, which the first review asked for. The sheet still does not say where the code is, and Best Software eligibility needs it on iGEM's GitLab. Add the address to this sentence as soon as the mirror exists.", "after": "Both interfaces run live on this page, and their source is at [GitLab URL] under the MIT licence.", "excerpts": ["software-2024-og-unm-01"], "excerpt_note": "UniMuenster's abstract names the platforms and how the tool is distributed, so a judge who will never install it still knows where it lives.", "ballot": "How well is the software written and documented for future groups to extend and improve? (Code documentation, comments in the code, performance evaluation, architecture diagrams, installation and execution scripts, etc.)", "zone": "abstract"}, {"id": "dt-a12", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "The loop is not closed, and almost every result rests on one run", "title": "Two loops share one word", "body": "Ending the abstract on its two biggest limits is the right call. On this page \"the loop\" is also the tubing the culture is pumped around, so a first-time reader can take this as a plumbing fault. Say which loop you mean: no decision is automated.", "after": "No decision is automated yet: a person still makes every call about the culture. Almost every result rests on one run.", "excerpts": [], "zone": "abstract"}, {"id": "dt-a13", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "26.7 h of warning before the OD channel failed", "title": "Nobody was warned during the run", "body": "The paragraph above says \"Replayed over the log\", and R3 says \"The alarm was computed afterwards from the log\". This row reads as if a warning arrived in time. Many judges read only the ledger, so put the replay in the row.", "after": "Replayed on one run's log, the alarm comes 26.7 h before the OD channel failed", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "How well was the software validated (e.g., through experimental work, test data, or benchmarking)?", "zone": "abstract"}, {"id": "dt-a14", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "24 h biomass within 7% on held-out arms", "title": "The held-out arms are plate arms", "body": "The row is called Plate-to-reactor prior, and the 7% comes from holding out one light arm of the same plate. R6 now says it plainly: \"whether it transfers to the reactor has not been tested\". Let the ledger say the same. R2 also says \"every OD on this page is an optical reading\", so \"biomass\" should be OD here.", "after": "24 h OD within 7% on held-out arms of one plate; not yet tested on the reactor", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "How well was the software validated (e.g., through experimental work, test data, or benchmarking)?", "zone": "abstract"}, {"id": "dt-002", "type": "praise", "anchor": "Without manual sampling, the reactor's own instruments are the only record of the batch, and they drift as they wear", "title": "The whole page argued in one line", "body": "This is the best sentence on the page. It gives the reader the problem (no sampling), the consequence (the instruments are the only record) and the catch (they drift) in one clause each, and everything after it is evidence for this line. Do not touch it.", "excerpts": ["software-2024-ug-vln-01", "measurement-2023-ug-wm-01"], "excerpt_note": "Both winners open on the difficulty instead of the tool. Vilnius: someone had to sit with the bioreactor. William-and-Mary: soil interferes with every fluorescence method. Your sentence does the same work in half the words."}, {"id": "dt-101", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "the twin's trust layer flagged the reference beam 26.7 hours before the OD readings became unusable", "title": "Say this was a replay", "body": "Read alone, this says the trust layer warned you during the run. R3 says \"The alarm was computed afterwards from the log; during the run nobody watched the trace daily and the fault was found late.\" The result is still a good one. Say it the way R3 does, so the introduction and the result agree.", "after": "On the 434-hour run the OD channel failed. When we replayed the log afterwards, the twin's trust layer flagged the reference beam 26.7 hours before the OD readings became unusable.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "How well was the software validated (e.g., through experimental work, test data, or benchmarking)?"}, {"id": "dt-102", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "From these it works out when to switch on green light to start protectant production, which protectant, and how much.", "title": "Present tense for a design", "body": "This paragraph and the one above describe the twin in the present tense. Section 2 says \"Today's rig software reads none of these channels\", green light is tagged planned, and your limits say every decision about the culture \"still goes through a person\". A judge who learns that in section 5 will reread the introduction as a claim. Say here what is designed and what runs.", "after": "From these the twin is designed to work out when to switch on green light, which protectant to make, and how much. None of that runs yet: today's software reads only the reactor, and a person makes every decision (Gaps and limits).", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "dt-005", "type": "praise", "anchor": "in-line A sensor on the loop of culture pumped around the bioreactor", "title": "A provenance word for every value", "body": "Tagging every channel in-line, commanded, soft, bench tested, planned or external lets a judge see at a glance which numbers are measured and which are hoped for, and you apply it in both tables. No winner we read has anything like it. The page now also says plainly that the log itself records a source for temperature only, which keeps the scheme honest.", "excerpts": ["software-2025-og-mun-01"], "excerpt_note": "Munich answers the standards question by naming formats a reader can check. Your provenance tags are the same kind of answer, and they are yours.", "ballot": "How well is the software compatible with, and does it leverage, existing synthetic biology standards? (e.g. SBOL, other RFCs, data formats, etc.)"}, {"id": "dt-105", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "eight pump settings from 10 to 24% in steps of 2, each measured once", "title": "How long was each setting held", "body": "This sentence gives the fluid, the temperature, the settings and the replicate count, and \"each measured once\" is exactly where a judge looks for n. Two things are missing for a team repeating it: how long each setting ran before the reading, and whether a reading is one sample or a mean over a window. Add them and R1 can be run from the page.", "after": "16 August 2026: water at 22 °C, eight pump settings from 10 to 24% in steps of 2, each held for [time] and read once as [one sample / the mean over a stated window], with the module fitted and again with a jumper in its place.", "excerpts": ["measurement-2024-og-kuleuven-03"], "excerpt_note": "KULeuven lists every concentration, the stock and the matrix in one sentence. R1 is two facts short of that.", "ballot": "Are the methods or protocols described well?"}, {"id": "dt-015", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "On 23 August 2026, cumulative TiO₂ additions gave 45 paired points between the in-line photometer and the BioDrop spectrophotometer", "title": "Half a protocol so far", "body": "The doses and the volume are now on the page, which the first review asked for. A team repeating this still cannot get the size of each addition, the liquid the TiO₂ went into, the blank, or the wait between adding and reading. The silica paragraph below gives step, range, volume and matrix in one sentence. Write this one the same way.", "after": "On 23 August 2026 TiO₂ was added to 500 mL of [liquid] in [mass] steps from 2 to 60 mg. After each addition the loop ran for [wait], then the in-line photometer and the BioDrop, blanked on [blank], read the same suspension. This gave 45 paired points, the last at 46 mg.", "excerpts": ["measurement-2024-og-kuleuven-03"], "excerpt_note": "KULeuven's curve can be remade from one sentence: eight concentrations, the stock, the matrix and why that matrix. That is the shape your TiO2 sentence is still missing.", "ballot": "Are the methods or protocols described well?"}, {"id": "dt-016", "type": "praise", "anchor": "Scoring both against the known dose, not against each other, showed that both degrade above 30 mg in different ways", "title": "Calibrating against truth, not an instrument", "body": "Scoring both instruments against a known dose is a better calibration design than anything in the six winning measurement pages, because it tests the reference instrument as well as yours. The clause \"not against each other\" is what makes it land. Keep it.", "excerpts": ["measurement-2024-ug-utexas-04", "measurement-2024-og-kuleuven-03"], "excerpt_note": "Austin calibrates to purchased standards and never scores the reference itself. You score both. That is the step past the winner.", "ballot": "Did the team validate and calibrate their measurement process appropriately for their approach?"}, {"id": "dt-106", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "while the photometer stays precise (R² 0.99, SD 0.022) and its response per milligram falls", "title": "Which instrument is the less linear", "body": "This sentence says the BioDrop \"keeps its slope but scatters\" and the photometer's \"response per milligram falls\". That makes the photometer the more precise one and the less linear one above 30 mg, and section 6 now agrees with \"the more precise of the two above 30 mg\". Measurement still says \"the BioDrop is slightly less linear than our own photometer\", and Hardware's bioreactor page calls it \"the slightly less linear of the two instruments\". Results has a word every page could share: \"our instrument is the steadier of the two\". Ask those two pages to use it.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Did the team validate and calibrate their measurement process appropriately for their approach?"}, {"id": "dt-017", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "Up to OD 1.2 the photometer regresses on the BioDrop with slope 1.018, R² 0.995, residual SD 0.024 OD", "title": "The ceiling is defended, the floor is not", "body": "Results gives this fit two things you leave out: \"over the twenty paired points where the BioDrop reads below 1.2, the fit is ours = 1.018 × BioDrop − 0.031\". Put the n and the intercept here. Then state the bottom of the range. Run 1 reads near 0.17, run 4 starts \"from near zero\" and the silica fit has an intercept of −0.07, so the low end is where a floor matters most.", "after": "Over the twenty paired points where the BioDrop reads below 1.2, the photometer regresses on it with slope 1.018, intercept −0.031, R² 0.995 and residual SD 0.024 OD. Below OD [number] a reading cannot be told from the blank, whose scatter is [number].", "excerpts": ["measurement-2024-ug-utexas-02", "measurement-2025-og-aalto-02"], "excerpt_note": "Austin states OD600 0.1 as the point its plate reader stops being trustworthy. Aalto reads its curve out loud at both ends. One line each.", "ballot": "Did the team validate and calibrate their measurement process appropriately for their approach?"}, {"id": "dt-107", "type": "cut", "anchor": "Measurement quotes the whole series, where both instruments fit above R² 0.999", "title": "A note between two of our pages", "body": "This paragraph explains to the judge why another ReLEAF page uses different numbers. Its first sentence is useful, and the result above already carries the same scope in \"Over the 16 points above 30 mg\". The rest is housekeeping between two teams. Fix the wording on Measurement and this paragraph can go.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "dt-020", "type": "praise", "anchor": "silica in 2.5 mg steps from 2.5 to 100 mg in 500 mL of 1% SDS, read at every step on the BioDrop", "title": "This one is a real protocol", "body": "Step size, range, volume, matrix, instrument, logging interval and duration, all in one sentence. A judge can repeat this. It shows you already know how to write the others.", "excerpts": ["measurement-2024-og-kuleuven-03"], "excerpt_note": "This sentence is doing the same work as KULeuven's calibration passage, which is the most copyable thing on any of the six winning pages.", "ballot": "Could the measurement(s) be reproduced by other iGEM teams?"}, {"id": "dt-108", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "Against the reference the current build is one to one to OD 2.5 (slope 0.995, intercept −0.07)", "title": "Name the two photometer builds", "body": "This section says \"both photometer builds\", \"the current build\", \"the 9:1 splitter build\" and \"the path runs 3 and 4 used\", and never says what the two builds are. Hardware calls them Complex (dual beam) and Slim (single beam). \"The reference\" also changes meaning: here it is the BioDrop, and in the next paragraph it is the reference beam. Name each build once and say which one was fitted for runs 3 and 4.", "after": "Against the BioDrop the dual-beam build is one to one to OD 2.5 (slope 0.995, intercept −0.07)", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Could the measurement(s) be reproduced by other iGEM teams?"}, {"id": "dt-022", "type": "praise", "anchor": "The run carries its own check. At 137.6 min the suspension was diluted and then restored", "title": "A check that needs no known dose", "body": "Diluting and restoring to test proportionality is clever, and the numbers are reported the way a judge wants them: three levels, each with its SD and its number of readings. Keep it, and copy the habit into R2 and R5, where the number of points behind each figure is still missing.", "excerpts": ["measurement-2025-ug-epfl-02"], "excerpt_note": "EPFL carries n, the correction and every p value in the sentence that makes the claim. Your dilution check already reads like that.", "ballot": "Did the team validate and calibrate their measurement process appropriately for their approach?"}, {"id": "dt-109", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "which is what the eight robust estimators are there to absorb", "title": "Eight estimators nobody has met", "body": "This is the only mention of \"the eight robust estimators\" on the page. A judge cannot tell what they estimate or why there are eight. One clause naming them, or a link to the photometer page, is enough. The finding is worth keeping: bubbles in 40% of readings and the reported value barely moves.", "after": "One bubble flag or more appeared in 40% of readings. The photometer's eight estimators ([what they are, or a link]) are there to absorb this; between OD 3.0 and 3.2 they agree within 0.002 OD, so the choice of estimator makes little difference to the reported value.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "dt-110", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "A check is an alarm when its score exceeds the 99.5th percentile of the healthy first 120 hours", "title": "What is the score, and why these hours", "body": "This sentence is what makes R3 a test: the threshold comes from one part of the log and is applied to the rest. Two things are missing. The page never says what the score is on either axis, so nobody can recompute it. And the first event starts at 120 h, exactly where the healthy window ends, on an alarm that was \"computed afterwards from the log\"; say whether 120 h was fixed before you looked at the later hours.", "after": "Each axis is scored every 10 minutes: the sensor axis as [statistic] of the reference lux, and the process axis as [statistic] of the specific growth rate. A check is an alarm when its score exceeds the 99.5th percentile of the first 120 hours, a window we chose [before / after] seeing the rest of the log because [reason].", "excerpts": ["software-2025-og-mrb-04"], "excerpt_note": "Marburg states its two pass criteria before giving the score, and says what was counted. Your threshold needs the same two things said out loud.", "ballot": "How well was the software validated (e.g., through experimental work, test data, or benchmarking)?"}, {"id": "dt-027", "type": "praise", "anchor": "A standard step detector (any jump above 0.05 OD) flagged 23% of sensor anomalies and 0.8% of biological shifts", "title": "You scored the obvious alternative", "body": "Not one of the eight winning software pages compares its tool against a simpler method. You run the naive step detector on the same data and report that it cannot separate the two kinds of event. That one sentence is worth more than the 26.7 hours, because it says what the trust layer adds.", "excerpts": ["measurement-2025-ug-epfl-02"], "excerpt_note": "EPFL proves its material works by comparing it against water, with the test and the p values. A baseline is the cheapest validation there is, and you have one.", "ballot": "How well was the software validated (e.g., through experimental work, test data, or benchmarking)?"}, {"id": "dt-030", "type": "praise", "anchor": "It has been shown on one run with one event of each kind; a detection rate needs deliberate faults on unseen batches", "title": "You name the experiment you have not run", "body": "This bounds the claim precisely and says what would settle it. Teams usually either overclaim or go quiet; you do neither. A judge reads this as a team that knows what its own evidence is worth.", "excerpts": ["measurement-2025-ug-epfl-03"], "excerpt_note": "EPFL says its own engineered protein cannot be confirmed statistically, then names the likely cause. Same discipline, and both teams won on it."}, {"id": "dt-112", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "On 10 October 2026 the field sensor rig was tested at three sites with stable air temperature", "title": "One paragraph doing five jobs", "body": "Opening R4 on the idea it shares with R3 was the right move. The same paragraph then carries the sites, the rig, the sensor, the method and the count of readings, in about 170 words. Break it after \"sit in the same air\" and start the method as its own paragraph. List the sites in the order the table uses, so the reader can match them.", "after": "On 10 October 2026 the field sensor rig was tested at three sites: an incubator at 22 °C, a stairwell at 26.4 °C and a room at 26 °C.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "dt-041", "type": "praise", "anchor": "Each site had three five-minute runs logged every five seconds, giving 540 paired readings.", "title": "Replicates and sampling rate, up front", "body": "Three sites, three runs each, a sampling interval and the total, in one sentence before any result. This is the section whose n sits exactly where a judge will look for it.", "excerpts": ["measurement-2025-ug-epfl-02", "measurement-2024-og-kuleuven-03"], "excerpt_note": "EPFL's n and KULeuven's listed concentrations do the same job. R4 is already at winner level on this question.", "ballot": "Could the measurement(s) be reproduced by other iGEM teams?"}, {"id": "dt-113", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "Sharing the same air, the two sensors should agree, yet the leaf sensor read 1.2 to 1.9 °C warmer once settled", "title": "Say which sites the range covers", "body": "Your table gives the room a site mean of +0.47 °C and a first run of −0.09, so \"1.2 to 1.9\" describes the incubator and the stairwell. The words \"once settled\" also meet the paragraph under Figure 7, which says \"Five minutes is not long enough for it to settle\". The range can stay as the headline. One clause naming the two sites, and one saying the room was still climbing, would stop a judge checking the table and finding a third answer.", "after": "Sharing the same air, the two sensors should agree, yet the leaf sensor read 1.2 to 1.9 °C warmer in the incubator and the stairwell. At the room site it was still climbing, from −0.09 to +0.97 °C across the three runs.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Did the team validate and calibrate their measurement process appropriately for their approach?"}, {"id": "dt-045", "type": "praise", "anchor": "Leaf wetness read 0.0% in every sample. The sensor was never wetted", "title": "A channel reported as untested", "body": "Reporting a flat zero and then saying the test cannot show whether the channel responds is harder to write than it looks. Most teams would have called 0.0% a dry reading and moved on. Keep both sentences; they are the ones a measurement judge will believe you on.", "excerpts": ["measurement-2025-og-aalto-03"], "excerpt_note": "Aalto reports a whole measurement as below the detection limit and excludes it from the arithmetic. Yours is the same refusal to let a number stand for more than it shows.", "ballot": "Did the team validate and calibrate their measurement process appropriately for their approach?"}, {"id": "dt-114", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "We compared Kalman filter, local linear, global linear and exponential forecasts against persistence, which predicts no change.", "title": "Five models in, two in the table", "body": "You compare five forecasts and the table reports two. A judge who wants to know whether the Kalman filter beats local linear has to read it off Figure 8. The table also needs its unit and its n: RMSE in OD, over how many hourly forecasts at each horizon. The tab label says \"rules fixed in advance\"; one clause on which rules were fixed, and when, would back that up.", "after": "At 2 h ahead the errors were persistence 0.067, Kalman 0.040, local linear [RMSE], global linear [RMSE] and exponential [RMSE], in OD, over [n] hourly forecasts.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "How well was the software validated (e.g., through experimental work, test data, or benchmarking)?"}, {"id": "dt-032", "type": "praise", "anchor": "A forecast was made every hour from 12 h, each using only data up to that point.", "title": "Walk-forward stated in one line", "body": "This sentence is what stops a reader asking whether the filter saw the future. It is one short line, and it makes the whole table believable. Judges look for exactly this clause and usually do not find it.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "How well was the software validated (e.g., through experimental work, test data, or benchmarking)?"}, {"id": "dt-115", "type": "praise", "anchor": "The 12-hour median OD rose from 1.06 to 3.75, so R5 forecasts the photometer reading, not biomass.", "title": "The limit sits beside the result", "body": "The first review asked for this caveat to move up from the limits list, and here it is, directly under the table. You say what the forecast is a forecast of, and your next sentence says what survives: the comparison holds because every model sees the same signal. A judge trusts the 41% more for it.", "excerpts": ["measurement-2024-og-kuleuven-02"], "excerpt_note": "KULeuven says what OD600 cannot distinguish in the section that uses the reading. You now do the same.", "ballot": "How well was the software validated (e.g., through experimental work, test data, or benchmarking)?"}, {"id": "dt-116", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "A logistic model beat logistic-with-lag by AICc", "title": "One sentence carrying six numbers", "body": "This sentence holds the model choice, a gloss of AICc, the fit error, a gloss of NRMSE, R², the criterion and three parameters. Each is worth having, and together they are hard to read. Split it into the choice, the fit and the parameters. K and X0 also need their scale, which is plate-reader OD.", "after": "A logistic model beat logistic-with-lag by AICc, a score that penalises the extra parameter. It fits at NRMSE 1.9% (normalised root-mean-square error; R² 0.997), against the 15% criterion for bioprocess models [3]. The fitted values are r = 0.299 ± 0.017 h⁻¹ (doubling time 139 min), K = 1.005 and X0 = 0.0235, the last two in plate-reader OD.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "dt-117", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "Across these six light arms, green induction costs no growth", "title": "Say what stands behind induction", "body": "The data show that green light cost no growth. A judge who follows the word \"induction\" to Measurement will read, of the Western blot from these samples: \"Neither scan in the draft carries a readable band, so neither is reproduced here and no expression claim rests on them.\" The sentence can stay as it is. Add one clause saying that expression in the green arms was not confirmed, so the two pages read as one account.", "after": "Across these six light arms, green induction costs no growth: r spans 0.279 to 0.317 h⁻¹, within one standard error, and the weakest-green arm reaches the highest plateau. Expression in the green arms was not confirmed, because the Western blot from these samples has no readable band (Measurement).", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "dt-037", "type": "praise", "anchor": "we left out each arm in turn, fitted the other five, and predicted its 24 h OD from its first 8 hours", "title": "Leave-one-out, with the table to prove it", "body": "Not one of the fourteen winning pages we read holds data back from its own fit. You do, you give all six arms, and you give the prediction with and without the prior side by side so the reader can see what the prior buys. This is the strongest single piece of evidence on the page.", "excerpts": ["software-2025-og-mrb-04"], "excerpt_note": "Marburg's structure benchmark is the closest any winner comes, and it has no held-out set and no comparison condition. Yours has both.", "ballot": "How well was the software validated (e.g., through experimental work, test data, or benchmarking)?"}, {"id": "dt-118", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "Observed OD at 24 h", "title": "What the observed column contains", "body": "Measurement's table for this run gives the red arms at 24 h as \"1.026, 0.969, 1.026\" with a blank of 0.059, so your 0.967, 0.910 and 0.967 are blank-subtracted. Measurement also says each green arm is three tubes and each red arm is one, and that the culture is \"WB800 + CcaSR\". None of that is on this page, and neither is the model behind \"Without the prior\". Put all four in a line under the table.", "after": "Observed values are the 24 h readings on Measurement minus the 0.059 blank. Each green value is the mean of three tubes and each red value is one tube, all of WB800 carrying CcaS/CcaR. \"Without the prior\" is [the model fitted to that arm's own readings to 8 h].", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Could the measurement(s) be reproduced by other iGEM teams?"}, {"id": "dt-123", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "It is the Arduino App Lab application our instructor Chars Hsieh wrote for the Uno Q board on BR-01", "title": "Who wrote the other pieces", "body": "Naming the author of the rig app is honest, and a judge will respect it. The page then stops: it does not say who wrote the rewrite, the trust layer, the forecasts or the figure scripts. A Best Software judge is scoring the team's software, so this is one of the first things they will want to know. One sentence here answers it.", "after": "It is the Arduino App Lab application our instructor Chars Hsieh wrote for the Uno Q board on BR-01, and it recorded every reactor number on this page. The rewrite was written by [name], and the trust layer, the forecasts and the figure scripts by [names].", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "dt-124", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "It has three parts. Firmware v3.10.9 (sketch/sketch.ino) runs on the Uno Q's microcontroller", "title": "Draw these three boxes", "body": "This paragraph is what the first review asked for: each part has a file, a place it runs and a job, and the last sentence says how they talk. The ballot names architecture diagrams, and the rig app's README already has one drawn in text characters. Redraw it as a figure here: three boxes, the Bridge link between two of them, and port 7000 out to the browser.", "excerpts": ["software-2024-ug-vln-02"], "excerpt_note": "Vilnius gives every component its name, its technology and a verb. Your paragraph now does the same, so the figure only has to repeat it.", "ballot": "How well is the software written and documented for future groups to extend and improve? (Code documentation, comments in the code, performance evaluation, architecture diagrams, installation and execution scripts, etc.)"}, {"id": "dt-125", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "The source is in software/rig-app/, with a README in Traditional Chinese.", "title": "A path needs a repository", "body": "The run route is new and it is concrete: one library with its version, one brick, one address. Two gaps remain. \"software/rig-app/\" is a path inside a repository the page never names, so a reader cannot clone it. And the only README is in Traditional Chinese, so for an English-reading judge this paragraph is the whole manual; say that, and treat it as the English summary.", "after": "The source is in software/rig-app/ of [repository URL]. Its README is in Traditional Chinese, and the steps below are the English summary.", "excerpts": ["software-2024-og-unm-02"], "excerpt_note": "UniMuenster tested its install guide on two operating systems and on a team member with no command-line experience, and says so. A route is only documentation once someone else has followed it.", "ballot": "How well is the software written and documented for future groups to extend and improve? (Code documentation, comments in the code, performance evaluation, architecture diagrams, installation and execution scripts, etc.)"}, {"id": "dt-126", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "Six tabs: what to do now, a live P&ID, the growth twin against its forecast band, weather and blast risk, the batch record, and setup.", "title": "Six tab names are not a walkthrough", "body": "Both interfaces run on the page, which is more than most winners offer. Neither has a walkthrough, so a judge opens the embed and has to guess where to click. Pick one task and number the steps. Write P&ID out as well: piping and instrumentation diagram.", "after": "A first look takes three steps. 1. Open the page full screen; with no board attached it runs on simulated data. 2. Open [tab] and read [what it shows]. 3. Open [tab] and [what to try].", "excerpts": ["software-2023-og-ins-02"], "excerpt_note": "INSA Lyon hands the reader its own experiment as a worked example, with every value to type in. Three numbered steps on your own simulated data would do the same.", "ballot": "Is the software user-friendly?"}, {"id": "dt-127", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "At 22 °C growth is 28 times slower but plateaus at OD 1.454, within noise of the 37 °C plateau", "title": "Give the numbers behind the comparison", "body": "No growth rate at 22 °C appears on the page, so a reader cannot see what \"28 times slower\" is measured against. The 37 °C plateau is not named either: run 2 held 0.84, the plate's K is 1.005 on the plate-reader scale, and run 4's temperature is not given. Give the two rates and name the plateau you compare with. It is also worth saying that 1.454 is above the OD 1.2 limit of the path it was read on.", "after": "At 22 °C growth is 28 times slower ([rate] against [rate] h⁻¹ in [which run]) but plateaus at OD 1.454, within noise of the 37 °C plateau of [value] in [which run], so the cost is time, not yield, and rate parameters do not transfer.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "dt-128", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "Any team with an optical sensor can use it; the 434 h batch comes with it as a test case.", "title": "Comes with it, from where", "body": "This is the page's best reuse offer and the reader cannot take it up. No file, script or command is named, and nobody outside the team is reported to have tried any of the seven items in this list. Name the script and the log, give the command that redraws Figure 4, and if another team or a classmate has run it, say who.", "after": "Any team with an optical sensor and a reference channel can use it. The script is [path], the 434 h log is [path], and [command] reproduces Figure 4 and the alarm at 216.3 h.", "excerpts": ["software-2023-og-ins-02", "software-2023-ug-fud-01"], "excerpt_note": "INSA Lyon gives the dataset and every parameter so a reader can rerun the team's own result. Fudan names two teams that used its tool. Those are the two strong kinds of reuse evidence.", "ballot": "Can the software be useful to other projects?"}, {"id": "dt-129", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "The board sends PV OD=0.412 PH=7.02 FLOW=263 PT1=2.69 PT3=1.31 PT4=0.45 PT5=0.35.", "title": "Fields without units or names", "body": "Showing the two lines is what the integration question asks for, and the heartbeat and timeout are explained well. The example still needs a key. It carries four PT fields while section 2 says the firmware reads three pressure transducers, and no field has a unit; if PT1 is in bar, 2.69 is above your 0.5 bar interlock. Add one line that names each field and its unit.", "after": "Fields: OD in [unit], PH, FLOW in [unit], and PT1, PT3, PT4 and PT5, which are [the transducer each one is] in [unit].", "excerpts": ["software-2024-ug-vln-02"], "excerpt_note": "Vilnius says exactly how the hardware and the server talk, component by component. Your two lines are the same idea and need only their labels.", "ballot": "How well can the software be integrated with external tools/software applications? (APIs, packages, etc.)"}, {"id": "dt-132", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "Trans-membrane pressure is read five times a second and logged, and a broken fibre shows up in it as an anomaly.", "title": "A detection claim the limits contradict", "body": "The reworded bullet is firmer than the one it replaced: it now says a broken fibre \"shows up\" in the reading. Your own limits say \"Trans-membrane pressure is below transducer resolution at the operating point, so lumen ΔP is used instead\", and no fibre has been broken on purpose to test it. Safety goes further: \"Real-time monitoring of transmembrane pressure ensures early detection of membrane integrity failure\". Write what is true today on both pages: the pressures are logged, and detection is expected and untested.", "after": "Trans-membrane pressure is read five times a second and logged. A broken fibre should show up in it as an anomaly; we have not tested this, and at the operating point the reading is below transducer resolution.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "dt-131", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "all of it will be mirrored to gitlab.igem.org/2026/software-tools/ before the wiki freeze", "title": "The eligibility line is in future tense", "body": "Best Software has two entry conditions: an OSI-approved licence, and source code \"hosted on the dedicated repository in iGEM's GitLab\". The licence is done. This sentence is the other condition, and it is a promise with an address that has no team name in it, beside a \"team repository\" the page never links. When the mirror exists, replace the promise with the link.", "after": "The rig app, the rewrite, the figure scripts and the numbers behind them are at [full GitLab URL], under the MIT licence.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "How well is the software written and documented for future groups to extend and improve? (Code documentation, comments in the code, performance evaluation, architecture diagrams, installation and execution scripts, etc.)"}], "excerpts": {"measurement-2023-ug-wm-01": {"id": "measurement-2023-ug-wm-01", "prize": "measurement", "team": "William-and-Mary", "year": 2023, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2023.igem.wiki/william-and-mary/measurement", "section": "Summary", "function": "page-opening", "text": "Measurement in soil synthetic biology experiments, compared to laboratory experiments in a flask or tube, faces many challenges in accuracy and quantitation. First, bacteria tend to attach to soil particles, and the opacity and autofluorescence of these soil particles interferes with fluorescence-based methods, such as spectrometry and fluorescence microscopy. Separation procedures are often unable to capture the whole bacterial community, and are biased towards specific taxa and metabolic states. Thus, our project focused on conducting multiple measurement approaches for each variable that was examined.", "why_it_works": "The opening is about why measurement is hard here specifically, and the strategy falls out of the difficulty rather than being announced.", "teaches": ["structure", "clarity", "evidence"], "prizeName": "Best Measurement"}, "measurement-2024-og-kuleuven-02": {"id": "measurement-2024-og-kuleuven-02", "prize": "measurement", "team": "KULeuven", "year": 2024, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/kuleuven/measurement", "section": "2.1 Introduction", "function": "limitation", "text": "Even though, the OD600 is widely used to assess bacterial growth characteristics, it has to be mentioned that this assay does have some disadvantages. Since OD600 measures cell density based on light scattering, both living and dead cells contribute to the reading. This means that while the assay provides insights into bacterial growth, it cannot directly assess survival.", "why_it_works": "A plain statement of what the reading cannot distinguish, placed in the section that uses the reading rather than in a limitations list at the end.", "teaches": ["assumed-knowledge", "evidence"], "prizeName": "Best Measurement"}, "measurement-2024-og-kuleuven-03": {"id": "measurement-2024-og-kuleuven-03", "prize": "measurement", "team": "KULeuven", "year": 2024, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/kuleuven/measurement", "section": "3.3.1 Standard Caliberation Curve", "function": "validation", "text": "To quantify the concentration of metal ions in bacterial samples, a standard calibration curve was generated using known concentrations of Cu ions (25, 50, 75, 100, 150, 200, 250 and 300 mg/L) . The standard solutions were diluted from a stock solution in 0.85% saline to ensure consistency with the sample matrix, as bacterial samples would also be suspended in saline.", "why_it_works": "The standards are made up in the same matrix as the samples, and the sentence says why, which is the difference between a curve that transfers and one that does not.", "teaches": ["evidence", "consistency"], "prizeName": "Best Measurement"}, "measurement-2024-ug-utexas-02": {"id": "measurement-2024-ug-utexas-02", "prize": "measurement", "team": "Austin-utexas", "year": 2024, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/austin-utexas/measurement", "section": "OD660 to Cell Count Standard Calibration Curve", "function": "uncertainty", "text": "Additionally, Figure 2B presents the same data plotted on a logarithmic scale, which helps to visualize lower particle concentrations. It highlights how the sensitivity of OD600 measurements decreases at lower particle counts around an OD600 value of 0.1. This indicates that our plate reader may not provide accurate readings for values at or below OD600 of 0.1.", "why_it_works": "They state where their own instrument stops being trustworthy and give the number, which turns a calibration curve into a usable range.", "teaches": ["evidence", "clarity"], "prizeName": "Best Measurement"}, "measurement-2024-ug-utexas-04": {"id": "measurement-2024-ug-utexas-04", "prize": "measurement", "team": "Austin-utexas", "year": 2024, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/austin-utexas/measurement", "section": "OD660 to Cell Count Standard Calibration Curve", "function": "assumption", "text": "Although particle count does value is not directly equivalent to cell count, an iGEM Interlab study found that “based on our results, we recommend calibrating OD to estimated cell count using serial dilution of silica microspheres, which produces highly precise calibration”5. Thus, we used the particle count per OD600 value as our cell count per OD600.", "why_it_works": "The substitution of particle count for cell count is flagged as a substitution and sourced, rather than being quietly folded into the conversion.", "teaches": ["evidence", "assumed-knowledge"], "prizeName": "Best Measurement"}, "measurement-2025-og-aalto-02": {"id": "measurement-2025-og-aalto-02", "prize": "measurement", "team": "Aalto-Helsinki", "year": 2025, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/aalto-helsinki/measurement", "section": "In-vitro Antibody Interference Measurement: Indirect ELISA", "function": "uncertainty", "text": "For example, our leftmost standard (0.1 ng/mL) gives us an idea of the lowest IF concentration that can be reliably detected above background. So, values that are much lower than this are not confidently distinguishable from background absorbance. Conversely, the highest standard (50 ng/mL) approaches the upper plateau of the curve, where the assay starts to saturate. As can be seen from the graph, the absorbance here is maximal; meaning higher concentrations will not give proportionally higher signals and should be diluted before being quantified.", "why_it_works": "The calibration curve is read out loud at both ends: the lowest point that beats background and the concentration where the assay saturates, with the instruction that follows from each.", "teaches": ["evidence", "clarity", "assumed-knowledge"], "prizeName": "Best Measurement"}, "measurement-2025-og-aalto-03": {"id": "measurement-2025-og-aalto-03", "prize": "measurement", "team": "Aalto-Helsinki", "year": 2025, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/aalto-helsinki/measurement", "section": "Elemental Analysis: Microwave Plasma Atomic Emission Spectroscopy (MP-AES) for Cobalt Quantification in Bound B12", "function": "uncertainty", "text": "MP‑AES analysis of nitric acid digests prepared from our intrinsic factor–vitamin B12 binding reactions produced cobalt signals below the reliable detection capability of method on the instrument used, with readbacks typically in the 20–30 µg/L range but a validated practical LOD of approximately 50 µg/L in this proteinaceous, acidified matrix; therefore, cobalt concentrations for all samples are reported as \"< 50 µg/L (LOQ)\" with no numeric values used in calculations or comparisons", "why_it_works": "A whole measurement came back under the detection limit and is reported as a censored value, with the readbacks shown and explicitly excluded from any arithmetic.", "teaches": ["evidence", "voice"], "prizeName": "Best Measurement"}, "measurement-2025-ug-epfl-02": {"id": "measurement-2025-ug-epfl-02", "prize": "measurement", "team": "EPFL", "year": 2025, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/epfl/measurement", "section": "Quantitative Analysis", "function": "evidence-with-numbers", "text": "To assess the contribution of individual components, partial formulations (cellulose alone; cellulose + silica) and a protein lysate control were compared against water (n = 3 each). None of these conditions differed significantly from water following Bonferroni correction (p = 0.1023 for cellulose; p = 0.4705 for cellulose + silica; p = 1.0000 for lysate).", "why_it_works": "A flat null result carries its n, its correction for multiple comparisons and each exact p value, so the reader can check the claim rather than take it.", "teaches": ["evidence", "clarity"], "prizeName": "Best Measurement"}, "measurement-2025-ug-epfl-03": {"id": "measurement-2025-ug-epfl-03", "prize": "measurement", "team": "EPFL", "year": 2025, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/epfl/measurement", "section": "Quantitative Analysis", "function": "limitation", "text": "Thus, under the current experimental design and limited sample size, the protective contribution of SRSF1 cannot be confirmed statistically. Notably, the lysate condition displayed a relatively large measurement error, which may reflect uncontrolled sources of variability such as fluctuations in wind or flame intensity.", "why_it_works": "The team's own engineered protein failed to beat the control and they say so in one sentence, then name the likely source of the noise instead of explaining the result away.", "teaches": ["evidence", "voice", "clarity"], "prizeName": "Best Measurement"}, "software-2023-og-ins-02": {"id": "software-2023-og-ins-02", "prize": "software", "team": "INSAENSLyon1", "year": 2023, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2023.igem.wiki/insaenslyon1/software", "section": "Example of use", "function": "method", "text": "To illustrate this, suppose you want to replicate the steps we performed to obtain the patterns used in our laboratory experiments. You can do this using the Git project you installed earlier.\n- First, access the dataset containing the \"oxa48\" gene in fasta format and an E. Coli genome.\n- In the Gene section, select the \"oxa48\" gene (JF388_29535).\n- In the Genome section, select the \"genome.fna\" file.\n- Check the box to display all patterns.\n- Select a size of 20 and a \"NGG\" motif.\n- Finally, select a mutation window from 13 to 16.", "why_it_works": "Hands the reader the team's own experiment as a worked example: the exact gene, the genome file, and every parameter value, so anyone can reproduce the result the wet lab used. Reuse is demonstrated by making one real run repeatable.", "teaches": ["reproducibility", "reuse", "evidence"], "prizeName": "Best Software"}, "software-2023-ug-fud-01": {"id": "software-2023-ug-fud-01", "prize": "software", "team": "Fudan", "year": 2023, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2023.igem.wiki/fudan/software", "section": "Overview", "function": "reuse", "text": "To make RAP user-friendly, we have designed an intuitive Web UI with tutorials; and for advanced usage of RAP, we have provided a series of RESTFul APIs. To compatible with existing sequence manipulation tools, we have adopted the GenBank format (opens new window) to save sequences, which is an existing standards, and the sequences can be used in external tools such as SnapGene (opens new window). In order to get feedback from external users to improve user-friendliness, we have collaborated with other iGEM teams (SCUT-China iGEM 2023 (opens new window) and Nanjing-China iGEM 2023 (opens new window)) to improve the software. Meanwhile, sequences predicted and generated by our RAP, has been validated by experiments (opens new window).", "why_it_works": "Four ballot questions answered in one paragraph, each with a named artefact: a Web UI and tutorials, RESTful APIs, the GenBank format and SnapGene, two named iGEM teams who used the tool and fed back, and wet-lab validation of its output. Nothing here is a claim without a referent.", "teaches": ["reuse", "evidence", "standards", "structure"], "prizeName": "Best Software"}, "software-2024-og-unm-01": {"id": "software-2024-og-unm-01", "prize": "software", "team": "UniMuenster", "year": 2024, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/unimuenster/software", "section": "Abstract", "function": "page-opening", "text": "Our tool allows access to machine learning assisted protein engineering in low-throughput settings. It can help you in case you too want to improve some protein’s activity, stability or any other characteristic, but are limited in your measuring capability to only few rounds of a small number of measurements. The command line tool works on Windows, Linux and MacOS and is distributed as an easy-to-install python package.", "why_it_works": "Written in the second person to the reader who might install it: the situation you are in, what the tool does about it, the platforms it runs on and how it is distributed. A judge who will not install anything still learns what it is and who it is for.", "teaches": ["audience", "clarity", "concision"], "prizeName": "Best Software"}, "software-2024-og-unm-02": {"id": "software-2024-og-unm-02", "prize": "software", "team": "UniMuenster", "year": 2024, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/unimuenster/software", "section": "Introduction", "function": "validation", "text": "We took a modular implementation approach for easy expandability and used the Typer library to bundle our software into a tool which can comfortably be used through the command line. Our modules communicate through easy-to-parse .csv files, so at any step it is possible to use some different tool and inject the results into our workflow. To ensure the quality of our tutorial we tested it both for Windows and Linux, and additionally tasked one of our team members, who didn’t have any python or command line experience, with following the installation guide and all the usage examples, to great success.", "why_it_works": "User-friendliness is tested rather than claimed: the install guide was run on two operating systems and then handed to a team member with no Python or command-line experience. The evidence is a person, named by their lack of expertise.", "teaches": ["validation", "audience", "evidence"], "prizeName": "Best Software"}, "software-2024-ug-vln-01": {"id": "software-2024-ug-vln-01", "prize": "software", "team": "Vilnius-Lithuania", "year": 2024, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/vilnius-lithuania/software", "section": "Introduction", "function": "page-opening", "text": "Monitoring and ensuring optimal bacterial growth conditions can often be a time-consuming task due to the long periods required for bacteria to grow and replicate. This was no exception in our wet lab activities, as our bioreactor required constant supervision and in-person presence in the lab. Additionally, identifying problems and when they occurred was challenging. To address this, we developed a remote interface for the bioreactor, reducing the time required for supervision and automating data collection by storing bioreactor readings in a remote database.", "why_it_works": "Opens on the lab problem, not the software: bacteria take a long time to grow, so someone had to sit with the bioreactor, and faults were hard to place in time. The tool is introduced as the answer to that in one sentence.", "teaches": ["clarity", "structure", "audience"], "prizeName": "Best Software"}, "software-2024-ug-vln-02": {"id": "software-2024-ug-vln-02", "prize": "software", "team": "Vilnius-Lithuania", "year": 2024, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/vilnius-lithuania/software", "section": "Architecture", "function": "method", "text": "- PostgreSQL Database: This relational database stores all bioreactor measurements and control settings, ensuring data persistence and easy access for both users and automated processes.\n- Python Flask Server: Acts as the backend, providing RESTful endpoints to read and write data to the database. Additionally, it handles automated processes such as sending alert emails when bioreactor readings exceed preset thresholds.\n- Arduino Client: The microcontroller in the bioreactor communicates with the system through REST APIs, sending real-time measurements (temperature, pH, turbidity) to the server and fetching updated control values like mixing speed and temperature settings. This integration allows the hardware to operate in sync with user inputs.", "why_it_works": "Each component gets its name, its technology and the job it does, and the microcontroller entry says exactly how the hardware and the server talk. An architecture diagram is readable in prose because every box has a verb.", "teaches": ["architecture", "clarity", "structure"], "prizeName": "Best Software"}, "software-2025-og-mrb-04": {"id": "software-2025-og-mrb-04", "prize": "software", "team": "Marburg", "year": 2025, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/marburg/software", "section": "Automated Structure Prediction", "function": "validation", "text": "Structures containing one of the remaining 24 donor units were evaluated using two criteria: The two T domains should be bound to opposite ends of the C domain and the Ppant arm substrates should extend into the C domain. 49 structures were correctly folded, representing 68 % of the 72 condensation complexes excluding split genes and epimerisation domains.", "why_it_works": "Gives the two pass criteria before the score, then the score with its denominator and the cases excluded from it. The 68 % is checkable because the reader knows what was counted and what was thrown out.", "teaches": ["evidence", "validation", "concision"], "prizeName": "Best Software"}, "software-2025-og-mun-01": {"id": "software-2025-og-mun-01", "prize": "software", "team": "Munich", "year": 2025, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/munich/software", "section": "Input-Output Pipeline", "function": "reuse", "text": "The tools output is composed of different files with customizable options. Industry-standard GenBank files compatible with all major molecular biology software including Geneious Prime, SnapGene and Benchling are the default. Each functional domain is properly annotated with feature tags for easy visualization and modification.\nAdditionally we provide the option of creating codon-optimized sequences for the target organism with avoided restriction sites for standard cloning methods these include: RFC10, RFC12, RFC1000, BioBrick standards, any custom restriction sites or a pre-assembled iGEM Registry Compatible Collection.", "why_it_works": "Answers the standards question by naming the formats and the standards by number (GenBank, RFC10, RFC12, RFC1000, BioBrick) and the three commercial tools the output opens in. Compatibility is shown as a list of names a reader can check, not as the word 'compatible'.", "teaches": ["standards", "reuse", "evidence"], "prizeName": "Best Software"}}};
